"Three rings for elven-kings under the sky,
	Seven for the dwarf-lords in their halls of stone;
 Nine rings for mortal men, doomed to die,
	One for the Dark Lord on his dark throne
 In the land of Mordor where the shadows lie.

"One Ring to rule them all, One Ring to find them,
	One Ring to bring them all and in the darkness bind them,
 In the land of Mordor where the shadows lie."

The One Ring was created by the Necromancer.  Among other things,
it makes the wearer invisible, keeps him from aging, and lets him
understand the languages of certain chaotic creatures (orcs,
Olog-Hai, Balrogs, etc.).  However, if worn long enough, it
changes the wearers alignment to "chaotic" and (eventually)
polymorphs him into an undead creature.  If a powerful enough
character wears it, he can telepathically sense those wearing
the other rings, and draw on their power for magic (very useful
in a fight).

The Necromancer, by the way, is looking for that ring.  If you
have it, he'll send his Ringwraiths after you (i.e. nine men
wearing the Rings of Power, which makes them all effectively
Master Liches, except for the Captain of the Nine, who is
powerful).  The only way to ditch them is to destroy the Ring by
throwing it in the Crack of Fire.  (If the Necromancer gets the
Ring back, you really, *really* lose.)

For further reference, see *The Lord of the Rings*, by 
Dr. J.R.R. Tolkien.
